to learn from the pattern that volume confirmation improves entry timing on catalyst-driven moves,

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:17.520
and we are adjusting position sizing rules accordingly. Now, here's something that most retail

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:24.420
investors get wrong, and it cost me money before I figured it out. Imagine you bought CNR last quarter

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:30.920
when the rail sector rotated out of favor, and your position now sits 12% below your entry. Between

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:37.660
your purchase and today, the account shows a time-weighted return of negative 4.2%, while money-weighted

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:43.940
return is negative 3.8%, because you added fresh TFSA contribution room mid-quarter.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:50.160
The difference matters for tax planning, because realized losses can be harvested before the superficial

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:56.580
loss window closes, while unrealized losses stay in the account for future recovery. Professionals

00:09:56.580 --> 00:10:03.560
always run the actual versus target allocation check first, then review position sizes against a 5%

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:09.680
single name cap, and calculate fees paid year to date. They also track the dividend income trend

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:16.900
line separately from price return, because compounding inside an RRSP or TFSA accelerates when payouts

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:23.400
grow. The biggest mistake retail investors make is skipping the realized versus unrealized split,

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:29.880
and missing tax loss opportunities. Instead, always run a simple checklist at quarter end.

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:36.380
Rebalance if any sector exceeds target by more than 3%. Harvest losses were appropriate,

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:40.740
and decide whether new capital goes to existing holdings or fresh ideas.

